PMC - PMC11959363
OAB - Digital health (DH) solutions, such as mobile health apps and wearable devices, 
      have the potential to improve population health in a scalable and cost-effective 
      way. However, the use of DH is uneven, with minority groups often facing more 
      barriers to adoption. This study aimed to understand why people do or don’t adopt 
      DH solutions and find ways to make them more accessible to everyone. Between 
      November 2022 and February 2023, trained community facilitators spoke with 118 
      people at public food centers across Singapore. Conversations explored people’s 
      awareness of DH, their experiences using them, and the challenges they face. Five 
      main themes were identified: awareness of what DH solutions offer, balancing 
      benefits against the effort required, accessibility, trust in the developers and 
      technology, and the importance of a positive user experience. Based on these 
      findings, we identified five strategies to improve DH adoption: (a) promoting DH 
      solutions through trusted community channels with digital literacy support, (b) 
      providing brief counselling in healthcare settings to help people see the value 
      of DH, (c) offering personalized rewards linked to individual goals, (d) ensuring 
      health apps are accessible and properly regulated, and (e) designing 
      user-friendly, engaging tools that motivate long-term use. These approaches could 
      help make DH more accessible and reduce health inequalities.
