MH  - Digital Health
PMC - PMC12700647
OAB - New behavior change theories, models, and frameworks (TMFs) are emerging to 
      support digital behavior change interventions like mobile apps, but their 
      adoption is slow compared to the speed of intervention development. In this 
      study, we compared the adoption of seven novel TMFs designed specifically for 
      digital interventions to the behavior change wheel (BCW) framework. We found that 
      the adoption of the novel TMFs is slow compared to the BCW, even after accounting 
      for their recentness. Challenges include a lack of awareness of these TMFs, a 
      lack of sufficient evidence to support them, and limited guidance on how to use 
      them to develop interventions. We recommend that to address these challenges, 
      researchers creating the TMFs should improve their design, provide detailed 
      guidelines for use, and provide initial evidence for the utility of their TMFs. 
      Other ways to address the challenges could include an online tool to guide 
      framework selection and further review work comparing the novel TMFs with 
      multiple traditional frameworks.
