TI  - Shifting the Paradigm: CAMBRA Adoption and Medical-Dental Integration Leveraging 
      an Electronic Health Record.
LID - 10.1002/jdd.70231 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE/OBJECTIVES: This study describes the integration of Caries Management by 
      Risk Assessment (CAMBRA) into a medical-dental integrated Epic electronic health 
      record (EHR) at Columbia University's College of Dental Medicine (CDM), with the 
      goal of incorporating risk-based caries management into predoctoral clinical 
      care. METHODS: A multi-phase, mixed-methods approach was used. CAMBRA protocols 
      were embedded into the Epic EHR, including clinical decision support, automated 
      scoring, integration with prescribing and documentation workflows, and patient 
      education after visit summary (AVS). Stakeholder engagement strategies included 
      faculty calibration, student education via a new Population Oral Health 
      Management (POHM) course, and staff training. Adoption was assessed through 
      caries risk assessment (CRA) completion rates and provider agreement with 
      algorithm-generated risk levels. RESULTS: Following EHR-integrated CAMBRA tool 
      implementation in July of 2022, CRA completion rates rose from under 5% to a peak 
      of 89% in March of 2024. Integration of the POHM curriculum and faculty 
      calibration efforts were key drivers of adoption. Provider agreement with 
      algorithm-generated caries risk levels exceeded 96% after a mandatory 
      confirmation field was introduced in November of 2023. CRA completion rates 
      varied by student groups, reflecting the importance of consistent faculty 
      leadership. CONCLUSION: Embedding CAMBRA within the EHR and aligning it with 
      educational and clinical workflows led to rapid adoption of risk-based caries 
      management. Success is driven by strong stakeholder engagement, curriculum 
      integration, and robust informatics infrastructure. Ongoing faculty calibration 
      and analytics feedback are essential to sustain performance. CDM's experience 
      offers a replicable model for implementing evidence-based caries prevention in 
      academic dental settings.
