TI  - Digital Health Adoption in Laboratory Services in Libya: Patient and Staff 
      Perspectives Using an Extended UTAUT Framework.
PG  - 1556-1560
LID - 10.3233/SHTI260470 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Laboratory digitalisation is seen as a way to reduce delays and 
      improve communication with patients. However, digital access to laboratory 
      results in Libya remains limited and uneven. This study examines adoption of 
      digital tools in laboratory services from both the patient and staff 
      perspectives, using an extended UTAUT framework that incorporates trust, cost, 
      literacy, culture and infrastructure stability. METHODS: A cross-sectional 
      mixed-methods design was used. Surveys were distributed to patients (both those 
      who had and had not received results digitally) and to 174 laboratory staff 
      across public and private laboratories. Quantitative responses were summarised 
      descriptively, and free-text responses were analysed thematically. RESULTS: Only 
      around one in four patients had received results digitally, although most 
      reported access to smartphones or the internet. Patients valued faster access and 
      fewer return visits, but expressed concerns about privacy, cost of data and 
      misinterpreting results without clinical explanation. Staff reported partial 
      digitisation, often using locally developed or "off the shelf" systems with poor 
      integration. Power outages, unstable connectivity, limited training and unclear 
      responsibility for releasing results to patients were common barriers. 
      CONCLUSION: By linking patient expectations with staff capacity, and by extending 
      UTAUT to include context-specific factors, this study shows that adoption is 
      constrained less by willingness than by reliability, support and governance. 
      Improving power stability, standardising software and protecting confidentiality 
      are essential for safe, patient-centred digital result delivery in Libya.
