OAB - By July 2020, many people were still ill weeks after they had had COVID-19, and 
      it was clear that existing National Health Service rehabilitation services would 
      struggle to help them all. We believed that an app could provide help and 
      reassurance with rehabilitation at home. We created an app with information and 
      treatments from a range of medical professionals to help patients do things that 
      could improve their symptoms. It also provided messaging between patients and 
      their clinics and helped healthcare professionals see information about many 
      patients easily. We aimed to recruit 1000 patients, but 7679 patients signed on, 
      from 33 clinics. Long COVID can make people feel very ill: as tired as people 
      with advanced lung cancer. Helping those patients manage tiredness is the best 
      way to help them get back to their normal lives. Also, patients with more 
      difficult lives feel more ill, so health services should make sure to reach them. 
      A varied team, including specialists from universities, medical services, 
      industry and patients, means you can develop an app quickly and effectively. How 
      you design an app and how you work with people to use it are both important for 
      people to accept it, and you need to involve patients and National Health Service 
      trusts and clinics from the start. Some people who might otherwise have refused 
      an app agreed to try because of lockdown. It helped patients to feel cared for 
      and helped clinicians manage a large workload. They might therefore be more 
      willing to use other apps in the future even though current record-keeping 
      arrangements might still put some clinicians off using apps.
