TI  - Provider-Related Determinants Influencing Drug-Drug Interaction Alert Handling in 
      the Electronic Health Record.
PG  - 418-422
LID - 10.3233/SHTI260189 [doi]
AB  - Drug-drug interaction (DDI) alerts are alerts in the clinical decision support 
      system (CDSS) that warn prescribers about potential DDIs. Unfortunately, most of 
      these DDI alerts are not acted upon, possibly resulting in inadequate alert 
      handling. While handling is often attributed to alert characteristics, prescriber 
      characteristics also seem to play a role in the handling of alerts. Thus, this 
      study aimed to identify provider-related characteristics of influence on the 
      handling of DDI alerts. The analysis of alert interactions in electronic health 
      record data resulted in the identification of seven determinants of influence, 
      namely: provider age, gender or role, internal or surgical specialty, in- or 
      outpatient setting, the mean number of orders placed per year and the mean number 
      of patients for whom orders were placed per year. The identification of these 
      determinants sheds light on prescribers' behavioral patterns and personal 
      characteristics that influence this behavior. These insights may contribute to 
      improved CDSS alert effectiveness, ultimately improving patient safety.
