TI  - Social Determinants of Potential eHealth Engagement Among People Living with HIV 
      Receiving Ryan White Case Management: Health Equity Implications from Project 
      TECH.
PG  - 1463-1475
LID - 10.1007/s10461-019-02723-1 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Evaluate the relationships between social characteristics of 
      Floridian persons living with HIV (PLWH) and both use of digital technologies and 
      willingness to use eHealth for HIV-related information. METHODS: Ryan White case 
      managers (N = 155) from 55 agencies in 47 Florida counties administered a survey 
      to PLWH (N = 1268) from June 2016-April 2017. Multilevel logistic regression 
      models were used to identify correlates of technology use and willingness. 
      RESULTS: Use of mobile phones with text messaging was high (89%). Older (vs. 
      younger) adults and non-Hispanic blacks (vs. whites) were less likely to use most 
      technologies. These groups, along with Hispanics (vs. whites) were less likely to 
      express willingness to use technologies for HIV-related information in models 
      adjusting for use. CONCLUSIONS: Among PLWH in Florida, eHealth-related inequities 
      exist. Willingness to engage in HIV-related eHealth is affected by social 
      determinants, even when considering technology access. Although eHealth may 
      reduce some healthcare inequities, it may exacerbate others.
