TI  - Barriers and Facilitators to the Collection and Aggregation of Electronic Health 
      Record HIV Data: An Analysis of Study Recruitment Venues Within the Adolescent 
      Medicine Trials Network for HIV/AIDS Interventions (ATN).
PG  - 168-176
LID - 10.1177/0163278721998413 [doi]
AB  - Electronic health record (EHR) data can be leveraged for prospective cohort 
      studies and pragmatic clinical trials, targeting youth living with HIV (YLH). 
      Using EHRs in this manner may minimize the need for costly research 
      infrastructure in service to lowering disease burden. This study characterizes 
      HIV prevention and care continua variables and identifies factors likely to 
      impede or facilitate EHR use for research and interventions. We conducted 
      telephone-based qualitative interviews with National Experts (n = 10) and Key 
      Stakeholders (n = 19) from subject recruitment venues (SRVs), providing care 
      services to YLH and youth at risk for HIV. We found 17 different EHR systems 
      being used for various purposes (e.g., workflow management and billing). Thematic 
      content analysis of interviews highlighted six broad categories of perspectives 
      on barriers to and facilitators of EHR use: specific variable collection, general 
      use barriers, and facilitators, general data collection barriers and 
      facilitators, EHRs for surveillance and research, EHRs for personnel and resource 
      management and capture of HIV specific variables. These findings may inform 
      implementation strategies of future studies, in which we conduct routine 
      monitoring of the youth HIV prevention and care continua using EHRs and test an 
      eHealth intervention.
