TI  - Perioperative Nurses' Experiences and Perceptions After the Implementation of an 
      Electronic Medical Record System: An Exploratory Qualitative Study.
PG  - e70276
LID - 10.1111/nhs.70276 [doi]
AB  - To explore perioperative nurses' experiences and perceptions after the 
      implementation of an Electronic Medical Record system in the operating suite at a 
      large metropolitan tertiary teaching hospital. An exploratory qualitative study. 
      Five face-to-face semi-structured focus groups were conducted with 20 registered 
      perioperative nurses employed at a major metropolitan health service in Victoria, 
      Australia. Data were collected between January and February 2024. Focus groups 
      were audio-recorded and transcribed verbatim, and data were analysed using Braun 
      and Clarke's (2022) framework for thematic analysis. Four key themes were 
      identified: (1) Adapting to Electronic Medical Records, (2) User experience, (3) 
      Design of the Electronic Medical Records, and (4) Delivery of patient care. The 
      implementation of an Electronic Medical Record system in the operating suite 
      elicited a mix of positive and negative responses from the participants. The 
      results emphasize a comprehensive patient data collection system that could be 
      further optimized to enhance efficiencies in specialized environments like the 
      operating suite.
