TI  - Moving electronic medical records upstream: incorporating social determinants of 
      health.
PG  - 215-218
LID - S0749-3797(14)00375-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.amepre.2014.07.009 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Knowledge of the biological pathways and mechanisms connecting social 
      factors with health has increased exponentially over the past 25 years, yet in 
      most clinical settings, screening and intervention around social determinants of 
      health are not part of standard clinical care. Electronic medical records provide 
      new opportunities for assessing and managing social needs in clinical settings, 
      particularly those serving vulnerable populations. PURPOSE: To illustrate the 
      feasibility of capturing information and promoting interventions related to 
      social determinants of health in electronic medical records. METHODS: Three case 
      studies were examined in which electronic medical records have been used to 
      collect data and address social determinants of health in clinical settings. 
      RESULTS: From these case studies, we identified multiple functions that 
      electronic medical records can perform to facilitate the integration of social 
      determinants of health into clinical systems, including screening, triaging, 
      referring, tracking, and data sharing. CONCLUSIONS: If barriers related to 
      incentives, training, and privacy can be overcome, electronic medical record 
      systems can improve the integration of social determinants of health into 
      healthcare delivery systems. More evidence is needed to evaluate the impact of 
      such integration on health care outcomes before widespread adoption can be 
      recommended.
